1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,890
For centuries, the world
called this country Persia.

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:08,300
Then, in 1935, its king asked foreign
governments to use a different name: Iran.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:10,820
He picked one date for the change.

4
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:13,180
March 21. The first day of spring.

5
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:18,823
Why that day? To find out, you need
three things: an empire, a poem,

6
00:00:18,843 --> 00:00:21,160
and a table set with 7 things.

7
00:00:21,890 --> 00:00:25,176
This is Iran. Nearly 90
million people live here.

8
00:00:25,196 --> 00:00:26,680
The capital is Tehran.

9
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:29,874
First, the empire. Around 550 B.

10
00:00:29,894 --> 00:00:35,200
C., a king named Cyrus rose in Pars,
a region in the south of today's Iran.

11
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,010
The Greeks called that region Persis.

12
00:00:38,030 --> 00:00:39,999
The Romans turned it into Persia.

13
00:00:40,019 --> 00:00:42,550
And the name stuck, for the whole country.

14
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:44,999
By Guinness's count, around 480 B.

15
00:00:45,019 --> 00:00:48,175
C. this empire ruled 44%
of all people on Earth.

16
00:00:48,195 --> 00:00:50,510
No empire has ruled a bigger share.

17
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:54,663
Its ceremonial capital, Persepolis,
still stands near Shiraz.

18
00:00:54,683 --> 00:00:58,600
On its great staircase, 23 nations
march up with gifts for the king.

19
00:00:59,370 --> 00:01:03,033
But here is the catch. Persia came
from the name of just one province.

20
00:01:03,053 --> 00:01:06,420
Inside the country, people used
another word for the whole land.

21
00:01:06,790 --> 00:01:11,026
That word is Iran. It is carved in
stone on a cliff near Persepolis,

22
00:01:11,046 --> 00:01:15,240
in the 3rd century A. D.. So the
name is at least 1, 700 years old.

23
00:01:16,290 --> 00:01:19,570
So how did the name survive?
Because the empire did not.

24
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:22,638
In 651, Arab armies ended
the last Persian empire,

25
00:01:22,657 --> 00:01:26,300
and much of the old Persian
culture was at risk of being lost.

26
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:30,976
Three centuries later, a poet named
Ferdowsi started writing a book.

27
00:01:30,996 --> 00:01:32,630
It took him about 30 years.

28
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:37,898
The Shahnameh, the Book of Kings, has
50, 000 rhyming couplets, in Persian,

29
00:01:37,918 --> 00:01:39,824
from legend to the last empire.

30
00:01:39,844 --> 00:01:43,260
It is the longest epic poem
written by a single author.

31
00:01:43,750 --> 00:01:47,126
Scholars credit it with keeping
the Persian language alive.

32
00:01:47,146 --> 00:01:50,140
And his tomb, in Tus, is
still a place people visit.

33
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:52,960
So far: an empire, and a poem.

34
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:56,030
The third thing is the one
that explains the date.

35
00:01:56,450 --> 00:02:01,939
Nowruz means new day. It begins at the
exact moment of the spring equinox,

36
00:02:01,959 --> 00:02:03,150
around March 21.

37
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:08,260
It is more than 3, 000 years old,
and UNESCO listed it in 2009.

38
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:11,135
First, families clean the whole house.

39
00:02:11,155 --> 00:02:15,424
On the last Tuesday night of the
year, people jump over small bonfires,

40
00:02:15,444 --> 00:02:17,860
to leave the old year's bad luck behind.

41
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:21,137
Then comes the table. The haft-seen:

42
00:02:21,157 --> 00:02:25,390
7 things whose names all start
with the Persian letter S.

43
00:02:25,410 --> 00:02:29,440
Usually sprouts, an apple,
garlic, vinegar, and sumac.

44
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:32,554
Each one is a wish. Sprouts for rebirth.

45
00:02:32,574 --> 00:02:35,260
An apple for beauty. Garlic for health.

46
00:02:35,770 --> 00:02:38,277
The new year lasts 13 days. On the 13th,

47
00:02:38,297 --> 00:02:41,520
almost the whole country
goes outside for a picnic.

48
00:02:42,030 --> 00:02:44,916
Now you can see why the
king chose March 21.

49
00:02:44,936 --> 00:02:47,690
A name Iranians had used for 17 centuries,

50
00:02:47,710 --> 00:02:50,550
asked of the world on
their oldest holiday.

51
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:53,256
Did the world obey? Not entirely.

52
00:02:53,276 --> 00:02:55,987
In 1959, his son said
both names were fine.

53
00:02:56,007 --> 00:02:59,500
We still say Persian carpets,
and the Persian language.

54
00:02:59,970 --> 00:03:03,616
If you go, you can follow this
whole story on the ground.

55
00:03:03,636 --> 00:03:08,460
Isfahan's great square, Naqsh-e Jahan,
is a UNESCO site, built around 1600.

56
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:11,254
And it is a country of extremes.

57
00:03:11,274 --> 00:03:15,690
Mount Damavand is a snow-capped
volcano about 5, 600 meters high.

58
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:17,253
In the Lut Desert,

59
00:03:17,273 --> 00:03:21,630
a NASA satellite once measured the
ground at 159 degrees Fahrenheit.

60
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:26,751
That was Iran. Which country
should we visit next?

61
00:03:26,771 --> 00:03:28,140
Tell me in the comments.

